00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,480
 but you will talk about it some more in engineering materials too.

16
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,640
 I'll go into approach to this the same way that I did with metals,

17
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,520
 talking about the mechanism that governs the elastic component of

18
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:45,440
 deformation, and then talking about the mechanism that governs the plastic component.

19
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,680
 But of course, you should note that in polymers,

20
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:51,520
 the elastic component and the plastic component occur at the same time,

21
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:54,720
 from very low stresses, completely unlike metals.

22
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,480
 Elastic deformation of engineering polymers occurs through two mechanisms.

23
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:01,600
 The first mechanism is kind of analogous to the way that metals worked.

24
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,520
 It's stretching of the polymer chains along their backbones.

25
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,400
 Here, the same way that atomic lattice planes were stretched apart

26
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,200
 in engineering metals under load and the metallic bonds

27
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,280
 provided some sort of restoring force.

28
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,800
 In an engineering polymer, the backbone elements are stretched apart,

29
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,840
 and their covalent bonds provide a restoring force.

30
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:22,960
 If load can be carried this way in a polymer through the chains,

31
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:26,400
 directly through the chains, they have a tendency to be quite stiff.

32
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:28,800
 This is because covalent bonds are relatively powerful,

33
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,440
 even compared to the metallic bonds that we saw in metals.

34
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:35,120
 Unlike metals, however, there's a second mechanism of elastic deformation

35
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:36,880
 that polymers have as well.

36
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:40,320
 Slip, elastic slip of the polymer chains pass each other.

37
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:43,680
 Remember in the last video, we talked about how polymer chains themselves are held

38
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,960
 together with covalent bonds, but the chains are held to each other

39
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:50,560
 primarily through van der Waals bonds, extremely weak bonds,

40
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,920
 kind of electrostatic wet pasta stickiness bonds.

41
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:56,720
 In order for any load to be carried through the bulk of a polymer,

42
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:00,800
 it needs to travel yes through each chain, but also between chains.

43
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,000
 And just like how stretching of covalent bonds inside of a chain provides

44
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,240
 a restoring force provides a stiffness,

45
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,920
 well, the van der Waals bonds between chains also provide a restoring force,

46
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,560
 also provide a stiffness, just a much weaker one.

47
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:15,200
 You should note that this elastic slip of the chains are recoverable.

48
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,400
 The same is the kind of stretch of the backbones of the polymer.

49
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:21,200
 The thing will return to its original length if you let the load off.

50
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,840
 But this is only true for sufficiently low loads.

51
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,160
 Because those van der Waals bonds are quite weak,

52
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:29,760
 they can only provide this restoring force for a very short amount of time

53
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:32,800
 before they overcome and plastic slip occurs.

54
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,800
 Talking only about elasticity though, these two mechanisms stretching over the chains

55
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:42,240
 and elastic slip over the chains work together to give you the box stiffness of the polymer.

56
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:45,520
 And because load needs to be carried through both the covalent bonds in a chain

57
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:48,880
 and the van der Waals bonds between chains, these two bonds,

58
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:52,880
 these two stiffnesses essentially act like springs in series.

59
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,160
 In this arrangement, the effectiveness, the box stiffness of the polymer,

60
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,600
 is highly dependent on its weakest sleeve, the van der Waals bonds.

61
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,760
 To put it another way, you can't utilize the fact that the covalent bonds that hold the carbon

62
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:08,400
 backbones of a polymer together are extremely stiff if the bonds between the polymer chains

63
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,880
 are weak. Those ones are just going to stretch first.

64
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:14,160
 This is one of the reasons why quite a lot of polymers aren't very stiff

65
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,280
 because they're mostly comprised of van der Waals bonds between chains

66
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,440
 and these van der Waals bonds aren't very stiff.

67
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:22,560
 That being said, there are some polymers which are reasonably stiff

68
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:25,040
 and this is largely owed to cross linking.

69
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,880
 In polymers that are highly cross linked, the van der Waals bonds are essentially taken

70
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,800
 out of the equation. Now, as before, load is carried through the covalently bonded

71
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,000
 backbones of the polymer strands, but it's also transferred

72
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,760
 between polymer strands through covalently bonded backbones.

73
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:43,200
 As a result, by increasing the amount of cross linking in your polymer,

74
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,760
 you essentially increase its stiffness. You take those van der Waals bonds out of the equation.

75
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:52,000
 While elasticity is complicated, plasticity of polymers is reasonably straightforward.

76
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,960
 Plastic defamation of engineering polymers corresponds to relative slip of the polymer chains,

77
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:00,240
 past what the van der Waals bonds can provide a restoring force to.

78
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:04,800
 Because these van der Waals bonds are so weak, this plastic defamation occurs pretty much immediately.

79
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:08,960
 It doesn't take very much load to overcome the van der Waals bonds and for the chains to begin

80
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:12,480
 slipping past each other. This is why, generally speaking, the yield strength of an

81
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:16,400
 engineering polymer is so low, essentially zero, why most engineering

82
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:21,520
 polymers begin plastically deforming alongside their elastic deformation immediately from very

83
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:25,440
 low stresses. Again, though there are exceptions to this rule, there are engineering

84
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:30,320
 polymers which do have a non-zero yield stress which do put up somewhat of a fight to plastic

85
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:34,480
 deformation. And again, these are polymers which are highly cross linked.

86
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,760
 While cross linking chains together, you essentially prevent this relative slip,

87
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,400
 you increase the yield strength of the polymer.

88
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:44,560
 And it turns out that very highly cross linked polymers actually begin to exhibit

89
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,360
 behavior that is somewhat elastoplastic. Because they're highly cross linked, these polymers

90
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:53,040
 end up having a reasonably high yield strength that don't begin plastically deforming

91
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:57,680
 until you begin to break these cross links. And consequently, you get some elastic behavior

92
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:02,400
 for a little while which can be quite stiff because again, cross links also increase stiffness

93
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:07,440
 before finally kind of overcoming these cross links in beginning to plastically deform the polymer.

94
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:11,520
 If you like cross links are a little bit like the strengthening mechanisms we met in metals,

95
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:15,920
 you essentially trade off ductility, you trade off capacity for plastic deformation

96
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:22,000
 in order to increase your strength. But unlike metals, doing so also increases stiffness.

97
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 We made a big song and dance in the early weeks about stiffness and strength not really being

98
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,320
 related, but cross linked polymers are an exception to that. The same mechanism which

99
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:35,920
 increases their stiffness cross linking also increases their strength and reduces their ductility.

100
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:40,080
 As a final point, I'll just note that I haven't told you the whole story. This behavior is

101
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:44,640
 actually a lot more complicated. Like metals, the mechanical properties of a polymer can change

102
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:49,360
 substantially under temperature, essentially corresponding to a weakening of these vendor walls bonds

103
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:54,000
 again. But in addition to that, the mechanical properties of a polymer can also change substantially

104
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,520
 as a function of whether or not a load is applied and how substantial that load is and how quickly

105
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:04,480
 that load is being applied as well. We generally call this strain and strain rate dependence of

106
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,680
 mechanical properties and it basically corresponds to two things. Number one, under load,

107
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:14,560
 the reasonably amorphous blob of polymer chains have a tendency to align with the direction

108
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:19,600
 of the load and carry more and more load along their carbon backbones. When this happens,

109
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:24,000
 when the polymers effectively become textured, more and more load is carried by the backbones

110
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,560
 and less and less through vendor walls bonds. This means that the polymers increase substantially

111
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:32,960
 in both stiffness and strength. The rate of load application matters as well,

112
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,440
 when loads are applied quickly, the random amorphous mess of polymer chains doesn't have time

113
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:43,120
 to nice and slowly unravel instead getting nodded and tied up. The net result is similar to cross

